            <title xml:id="title_cb7c1dcd" xml:lang="de">An dem hohen Geburtsfeste der durchlauchtigsten Fürstin und Frau Louise vermählten Kronprinzeßin ward folgende Cantate aufgeführet</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_d6bfae63">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_f6c49c94">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_a4a59584">Johann Elias Schlegel</persName>
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_c91dc620">Crown Princess Louise</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_7dbfae07">
               <date isodate="1743" xml:id="date_6850f9ef">1743</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_f1053ef3">Copenhagen,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_a61c1c17">
               <p xml:id="idm184">The cantata was composed for the official birthday celebration of the newly arrived <persName xml:id="idm185" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Princess Louise of Great Britain</persName>. The performance took place on 18 December. Though the name of the author does not appear on the printed libretto, it was <persName xml:id="idm186" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Elias Schlegel</persName>. He had arrived in Copenhagen earlier that year as secretary to the Saxon envoy, and one of his first assignments was to write the following text. Before he took up employment in Copenhagen, Schlegel had corresponded with <persName xml:id="idm187" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Christoph Gottsched</persName> and received a letter of introduction so that through Scheibe he could present himself to <persName xml:id="idm188" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Hans Gram</persName> and hence gain access to The Royal Library. Schlegel writes to <persName xml:id="idm189" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich von Hagedorn</persName> that he unfortunately only possessed very few books and had to borrow some German ones from Scheibe. Schlegel and Scheibe became close friends and collaborated on several occasions.</p>
            </history>
